**Change 3 — Add the onSnapshot listener** (add immediately after the query block, around line 762):

```typescript
// ADD this useEffect right after the useQuery block:
useEffect(() => {
  // Listen to the tiny signal document — fires only when a class goes LIVE or ENDS
  const unsub = onSnapshot(
    doc(db, 'live_class_index', 'current'),
    (_snap) => {
      // Backend just wrote a status change — invalidate and re-fetch the list
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['studentDashboard'] });
    },
    (error) => {
      // Firestore permission error or offline — silently ignore, page still works
      // (students will still see data from last successful fetch)
      console.warn('[LiveDashboard] Firestore signal listener error:', error.code);
    }
  );

  // Cleanup on component unmount (navigating away from page)
  return () => unsub();
}, [queryClient]); // queryClient is stable — this effect runs once on mount
```

**Why the error handler?** If a student has no internet for a moment, or if Firestore rules
block them (shouldn't happen, but defensive), the page still works — they just won't get
the push update. The existing `refetchOnWindowFocus: true` still saves them when they come back.

